From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 21:21:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix: keep each store's key under the profile's KEK, and never
 raze it

Moved under a password KEK by an embedder's secret, the profile's keys
were asked for by the KEK each store had made them under, by name, and
were not found. On the S9 (encriox, stage 2's second round), with
the secret given, every SQLite open failed at the next start: the
cookie store moved cookies.sqlite aside as corrupt, and site storage
could not start. The HTTP cache deleted its moved key and made a new
one, back under a LocalKey, losing every entry.

Each store now reads its key under whichever KEK wraps it, and a new
key goes under the profile's password KEK once there is one: SQLite's,
the HTTP cache's and QuotaManager's anchor. A key that is there and
locked is kept, never replaced. A database whose key is locked fails to
open with NS_ERROR_FILE_ACCESS_DENIED, as obfsvfs fails an open it
cannot key, which no consumer takes for corruption; NS_ERROR_FAILURE is
one the cookie store answers by moving its database aside. A session
whose keys were locked from startup does not use the disk cache, since
reading an entry without its key truncates it and the index's rebuild
deletes what it cannot read.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
